    "product_id": "19c7547d-9287-4cca-a7d3-9138a51ef04c",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 030504 CCA\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion \nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV \n1005 PM PDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Drier, cooler, and breezy conditions expected through the work \n  week.\n  \n* Storm chances return this weekend with higher rain potential  from \n  monsoonal push and possible remnants of Tropical Storm Marie.\n  \n&&\n\n\n.DISCUSSION...Today through Tuesday.\n\nMostly clear skies and a cooling trend continues today with highs \naround 5 degrees below average. Aside from gusty winds (with peak \ngusts around 30mph) the forecast remains fairly calm and dry with \ndew points falling to around 10 degrees and RH being less that 10 \npercent. Thursday and Friday will continue the relatively cool \nconditions (yes, highs in the 90s are relatively cool in the valley \nthis time of year) with monsoonal influence beginning to add some \nslight improvement to humidities into the area. Winds will increase \nover the next few days with higher terrain and favored gaps beginning \nto see gusts above 40 mph Thursday and Friday afternoon.\n\nThe increasing winds and building moisture look to fuel storm \nchances over the weekend. Some mid to longer term models also show a \nportion of the remnants from Tropical Storm adding tropical moisture \nto the mix over the weekend. These ingredients will combine to offer \nstrong chances for fairly widespread showers and storms on Saturday \nwith increased chances on Sunday as of the most recent forecast \nupdates. While there is still some changes possible for this portion \nof the forecast, you may still want to prepare for hazardous weather \nfor the holiday weekend, especially Sunday. Storms will offer plenty \nof lightning, rain, and strong gusts. Shower chances will be more \nwidespread and with increased the PWATs over the weekend, could \nprovide heavy rainfall, leading to flood risks. Adding to the risk \nof flooding will be the potential for storm and shower tracks to \ntrain over portions of the region, leading to persistent rain fall. \nRain chances reduce into Labor Day, but linger into the next work \nweek.\n&&\n\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 06Z Forecast Package...\nAnother night of elevated south-southwesterly wind gusts expected. \nAdded wind gusts to the prevailing TAF from 08 to 14Z, with \nsustained speeds between 10 and 15 kts and gusts 20 to 25 kts, \nthough there is low-to-moderate confidence in the timing and \npersistence of overnight gusts. Gusts will return Thursday afternoon \nwith similar speeds. No operationally significant cloud cover \nthrough the TAF period. Temperatures will remain below 100 degrees.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Generally south-\nsouthwest winds will continue each afternoon through the TAF period, \nwith gust speeds between 20 and 30 kts. Sustained winds will remain \nelevated overnight between 10 and 15 kts. The exception will be KDAG \nwhere gusty west winds can be expected. No operationally significant \ncloud cover through the TAF period.\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Murdock\nAVIATION...Soulat\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
